{
  "skill": "cascade",
  "purpose": "Frozen test inputs for /prompt-lab autoresearch loop. Hold constant across iterations so score deltas reflect prompt quality, not input variance.",
  "topics": [
    {
      "topic": "How Claude's extended thinking mode changes debugging",
      "transcript": "When you hit a bug in production, your first instinct is to look at the error message. It says 'null pointer exception' or 'timeout.' So you start digging. You check the logs. You add more logging. You run it locally. Thirty minutes later, you finally see what broke. But what if Claude could see it in the first ten seconds? Extended thinking is Claude's reasoning step made visible. Instead of just giving you the answer, it shows you the path to the answer. For debugging, that changes everything. You paste the error, the code, and the context. Claude reasons through exactly what's happening, step by step, before it gives you the fix. The error becomes obvious because you see the reasoning. We tested this with three real production failures: a race condition in a cache layer, a memory leak in a service, and a subtle latency issue. In each case, extended thinking caught the root cause faster than traditional debugging. The reason is simple: extended thinking makes your debugging process externally visible. You're not fighting the black box anymore. You're watching the thinking that leads to the solution. For knowledge workers, this is a superpower."
    },
    {
      "topic": "The hidden costs of async/await in Rust",
      "transcript": "Tokio is beautiful. Async Rust lets you write concurrent code that feels almost synchronous. For a few hundred thousand requests per second, it's the right tool. But production Rust shops hit three walls that nobody talks about. First: Future trait objects hide allocations. When you box a Future, you're allocating on the heap. Small allocations add up. In a hot loop running millions of times, those allocations are your latency spike. The Tokio book doesn't teach you how to spot this. You only see it in the profiler. Second: executor starvation. If you mix CPU-bound work with async tasks, you block the executor. Other tasks wait. Your latency explodes. The fix is simple — spawn_blocking for CPU work — but most tutorials skip it. Third: panic behavior across task boundaries is weird. A panic in a spawned task doesn't panic your main thread. It silently fails. Your error handling becomes invisible. We built three production services before understanding this. Each failure taught us something the documentation should have mentioned. The lesson is simple: async/await in Rust is powerful and performant. But if you don't understand these three costs, you'll chase ghosts in production."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Why local-first databases are finally winning",
      "transcript": "For fifteen years, we built everything on cloud databases. You'd build an app, sync it to the server, the server becomes the source of truth. Your user is offline? They wait. Network is slow? They wait. The user experience felt like the internet had a latency problem. But the real problem was the architecture. Enter local-first: your database lives on the user's device. It's always available. It syncs when it can. The user never waits. For years, this was research. Then SQLite went to WebAssembly. Replicache solved conflict resolution. CRDTs made merging state trivial. Suddenly local-first became practical. We've built two products this way now. The difference in user experience is stunning. Instant interactions. Offline works. Real-time collaboration without the server complexity. The tradeoff is simple: you need to think about sync and merging. But modern tools make that easier every day. The economics have flipped. Cloud-first costs you user experience. Local-first gets you there with less server cost."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Context switching as the productivity killer",
      "transcript": "You think you had a productive day. But actually, you got interrupted forty-seven times. Slack, email, meetings, browser tabs. Forty-seven little context switches. The cost is brutal. Research shows each switch costs fifteen to twenty-five minutes of recovery time. So those forty-seven interruptions don't cost forty-seven minutes. They cost eight to twelve hours of actual recovery. You can't even see it happening. You think it's just a bad day. But the system is killing you. Here's what changed for us: we measured it. We audited a day. Calendar blocking. Interrupt patterns. Tool notifications. The picture was horrifying. So we restructured work around batching. Async-first communication. Decision protocols that don't require synchronous consensus. Turned context-switching from constant to occasional. Productivity doubled. Not because we worked harder. Because the system stopped murdering our focus. The beautiful part: this scales to teams. If your team is context-switched constantly, you're destroying leverage. Fix the system, fix the output."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Building AI agent orchestration without spaghetti",
      "transcript": "Two AI agents are easy. One researcher, one writer. You wire them together in LangChain. Works great. Then you add a third agent. A reviewer. Now you need orchestration. Where does the researcher output go? Does the writer see it? Does the reviewer give feedback to the writer or the researcher? Suddenly complexity exploded. Five agents later, your message passing looks like spaghetti. Every agent touches every other agent's state. Debugging is a nightmare. We've built a few agent systems now. The pattern that works is this: agents are stateless. All state lives in a message bus or event log. Agents are subscribers. They pick up work, do their job, emit results. A supervisor orchestrates the flow. No direct agent-to-agent communication. This scales. You can add agents without refactoring. You can replay the log. You can debug by reading the event stream. The reason it works: you've separated concerns. Agents do their one job. The system orchestrates. Most frameworks don't teach this. They make it too easy to couple agents together. If you want to scale beyond toys, think about this pattern first."
    },
    {
      "topic": "How to benchmark AI models on your actual use case",
      "transcript": "Claude scores higher on MMLU than GPT-4. Benchmarks love Claude. But on your production workload? You won't know until you measure it right. Here's the mistake most teams make: they test on random data or toy examples. But your real workload has quirks. Maybe you care about latency. Maybe you need consistency. Maybe you're doing something MMLU never tests. So here's the process we use: define your test set. Real examples from production. At least fifty, ideally two hundred. Create a rubric. What does 'correct' mean for your use case? Not general knowledge. Your specific problem. Test both models on that set. Same temperature, same format, same everything. Score each response using the rubric. Calculate win rate. Include cost and latency. Now you can make a decision. Is Claude ten percent better but costs fifty percent more? Is GPT faster but less accurate? These tradeoffs are real. They're in the spreadsheet. The reason benchmarks fail: they measure general capability. You care about specific capability. Bridge that gap and you'll make smarter model choices."
    },
    {
      "topic": "The economics of local LLM vs renting Claude",
      "transcript": "Running your own LLM is cheap. Llama 3.1 on a GPU costs fifty dollars a month. Claude API costs five hundred. So obviously you should run local, right? Not so fast. There are hidden costs nobody talks about. Infrastructure cost is the visible line. Then you have compute ops. Who maintains the server? Who optimizes the inference? Who handles the model updates? That's your team's time. Suddenly fifty dollars a month looks like three thousand dollars a month. Then there's latency. Claude responds in two seconds. Your local setup might take ten. Is that acceptable? Maybe. Maybe not. Fine-tuning cost is interesting too. If you're optimizing a model for your domain, that's development time. With Claude, you just prompt-engineer. Other variable is integration risk. Llama's API changes. Your deployment breaks. Claude's doesn't. You're trading capital expense for operational expense and complexity. The right choice depends on your constraints. Huge throughput? Local wins. Small consistent traffic? Claude wins. Long tail workloads? Local with Claude fallback wins. This is why most teams use both. They're not in competition. They're different tools for different needs."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Fitness habit formation through optionality-based systems",
      "transcript": "Rigid workout schedules fail ninety percent of people. Here's why: life is unpredictable. Unexpected meeting. Sick day. Travel. Your perfect Monday-Wednesday-Friday schedule breaks. Then willpower fails. You miss one day. Guilt. You don't go back. The system failed because it was brittle. But there's a different approach: optionality. Instead of prescribing exactly what you do, you define a menu of activities at different intensities. Busy day? Do the ten-minute flow. Open afternoon? Do the strength work. Traveling? Do the bodyweight version. The commitment is consistent: move every day. The form is flexible. This removes the psychological failure mode. You're never failing the system. You're choosing which version fits today. We've tested this with dozens of people. Adherence jumps dramatically. The reason is psychological: you have agency. You're not following orders. You're choosing how to honor the commitment. Motivation doesn't matter anymore because the system doesn't require it. You just pick the thing that fits. That's how sustainable change happens."
    }
  ],
  "model_settings": {
    "temperature": 0.7,
    "max_tokens": 2000,
    "seed": 42
  },
  "notes": "8 transcripts, each 350-500 words, simulating finished long-form video scripts (intro, 2-3 content beats, implicit CTA). Spans AI/tech, productivity, fitness. Tests whether cascade.py generates platform-appropriate repurposing (blog, LinkedIn, Instagram) from varied content styles."
}

